{
  "_comment": "FormalShell pywal template. Not run by the shell, pywal renders it, you wire the output up yourself: drop this file at ~/.config/wal/templates/pywal-theme.json, run `wal -i <image>`, pywal writes the rendered copy to ~/.cache/wal/pywal-theme.json, then symlink (or a `wal -o` post-run hook copies) that file to $XDG_STATE_HOME/formalshell/theme.json (usually ~/.local/state/formalshell/theme.json), FormalShell's Theme.qml watches that path and re-themes live on change. Pywal's 16-color ANSI palette can't express all of shadcn's roles as distinctly as matugen's Material roles (theme.json.tmpl); this mapping follows the common pywal convention (color4 as primary, color8 as the muted-ink tone, color1/color3 as destructive/warning) and reuses color0 for the near-background fills (card, border, popover, secondary, muted, accent) that all sit close to background. Pywal has no light/dark template variable, so mode below is hardcoded dark, hand-edit it to \"light\" after generating with `wal -l`. This file must stay valid JSON after pywal's substitution, this comment is a JSON field for that reason, not a // line.",
  "mode": "dark",
  "background": "{background}",
  "foreground": "{foreground}",
  "card": "{color0}",
  "cardForeground": "{foreground}",
  "popover": "{color0}",
  "popoverForeground": "{foreground}",
  "primary": "{color4}",
  "primaryForeground": "{background}",
  "secondary": "{color0}",
  "secondaryForeground": "{foreground}",
  "muted": "{color0}",
  "mutedForeground": "{color8}",
  "accent": "{color0}",
  "accentForeground": "{foreground}",
  "destructive": "{color1}",
  "destructiveForeground": "{background}",
  "warning": "{color3}",
  "warningForeground": "{background}",
  "border": "{color0}",
  "input": "{color0}",
  "ring": "{color4}",
  "chart1": "{color4}",
  "chart2": "{color0}",
  "chart3": "{color3}",
  "chart4": "{color0}",
  "chart5": "{color8}"
}
